--------------------------------- KEY POINTS ---------------------------------

✓ In-place merge (reuses existing nodes, no new allocation)
✓ Maintains sorted order by always picking smaller node
✓ Handles unequal length lists (attaches remaining)
✓ Handles empty lists (edge cases at start)
✓ O(m + n) time - single pass through both lists
✓ O(1) space - only pointers used


//////////////////////// QUESTION ////////////////////////////

Merge Two Sorted Linked Lists

Given the heads of two sorted linked lists, merge them into a single sorted linked list and return its head.

Examples:

Example 1:
Input:  List1: 5 -> 10 -> 15 -> 40
        List2: 2 -> 3 -> 20
Output: 2 -> 3 -> 5 -> 10 -> 15 -> 20 -> 40

Example 2:
Input:  List1: 1 -> 4
        List2: 1 -> 2
Output: 1 -> 1 -> 2 -> 4

Constraints:
- 1 ≤ list1.size, list2.size ≤ 10³
- 0 ≤ node->data ≤ 10⁵

Difficulty: Medium | Accuracy: 62.91% | Points: 4

Approach: Two-Pointer Merge
- Compare heads of both lists
- Attach smaller node to result
- Move pointer of chosen list forward
- Attach remaining nodes when one list exhausts

Time: O(m + n) | Space: O(1)
